those who serve in boats like this one  call themselves the silent service  but a new nuclear-powered submarine pact  between the uk the us and australia has  triggered a noisy reaction from china  it severely damages regional peace and  stability intensifies the arms race and  jeopardizes international efforts  promoting the non-proliferation of  nuclear weapons  this is a highly irresponsible act  the three partners say building a new  submarine fleet for australia and  collaborating more broadly in security  will bolster their defenses in the  indo-pacific and beyond  but it's also bruised a key ally  a french submarine contract with  australia now sunk  it is really a stab in the back we built  a relationship of trust with australia  and this trust has been betrayed and i'm  angry today and bitter about this breach  this is not done between allies  especially when there's been two years  of negotiations for this contract  at an arms fair in london the defense  secretary said he sympathized with what  he described as france's disappointment  and he dismissed chinese allegations of  the three allies having a cold war  mentality  it's a cold war allegation to say that  it's a cold war it's not a cold war this  is simply uh the exercise a country that  shares our value exercising its desire  to keep strategically one place ahead to  make sure its defenses are strong that  there's nothing wrong with that there's  nothing provocative  reacting to news of the deal in the  commons not everyone seems so sure what  are the implications of this pact for  the stance that would be taken by the  united kingdom and for its response  should china attempt to invade taiwan  big questions left hanging questions 2  for france also selling its wares at the  defense fair  the focus is on submarines but this new  security alliance is all about western  allies teaming up in the face of what's  seen as an existential challenge posed  by china the hope on the uk side is that  the rift with france will fade because  ultimately they share the same values  and right now western powers need to  stick together  but it's a tension that rivals like  china could well seek to exploit  debra haynes sky news
 

